{"abstract":[{"text":"A type that wraps a base graph and passes its positions through.","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"GraphAdaptor"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A7AdaptorP4BaseQa","text":"Base","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/GraphAdaptor\/Base-swift.associatedtype"},{"kind":"text","text":">"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"overview","text":"Overview","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Views (a transposed view, a subgraph view, a directed or undirected view) and wrappers that add"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"rules (a multigraph, an acyclic graph) are adaptors. Every adaptor gets two things:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Its ","type":"text"},{"code":"generation","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the base’s, so a structure derived from the adaptor is current exactly","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"when one derived from the base would be, and a generation read from either is current for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"both, although they may describe different graphs.","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"When the base is a "},{"code":"PropertyGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"values(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"[map, of: vertex]","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" read the base’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex maps. When the adaptor’s edge positions are the base’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"("},{"code":"Edges.Index == Base.EdgePosition","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"), "},{"code":"[map, ofEdgeAt: position]","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"values(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" read the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"base’s edge maps too. A view whose positions are not the base’s, such as a complement, has no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge-map reads. These members are read-only. A "},{"code":"GraphDelegator","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" writes maps through members"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of its own."}]}]}],"type":"unorderedList"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A view whose vertices are a subset of its base’s should declare its own "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[map, of: vertex]"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that traps on a vertex outside the view. The default reads the base’s value."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Nothing else is forwarded. A view’s graph members are its own, or the protocol defaults over","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"its own members. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GraphDelegator"},{"text":" forwards every read member to the base, and only a wrapper","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"that keeps the base’s adjacency conforms to it. For a view such as a transposed one, the base’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"members would describe the wrong graph.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"relationshipsSections":[{"type":"inheritedBy","title":"Inherited By","kind":"relationships","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/GraphDelegator"]},{"type":"conformingTypes","title":"Conforming Types","kind":"relationships","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedView","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/UndirectedView"]}],"kind":